course: dictionary
lesson: 7248
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them show which meaning is used, and that is the context. «They always go to the servo for a pie, just the two of them.» You buy a pie at a petrol station and eat it, so here pie means food.
explain: Pie can also mean a pie chart, a round graph cut into slices that show parts of a whole. Look for clues like chart, slices or percentages to know which meaning fits.
explain: Pi (π) sounds the same as pie but is a different word. It is the number about 3.14 used for circles. Put your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: A word with more than one meaning can be understood by looking at the words around it.
fact: A pie is food made of pastry filled with fruit, meat or vegetables and baked in an oven.
fact: A pie chart is a round graph cut into slices that show parts of a whole.
fact: «Pi» (π) is a different word that sounds the same, and it is the number about 3.14 used for circles.
fact: «As easy as pie» is a phrase that means something is very easy to do.
exercise: «They always go to the servo for a pie, just the two of them.» What is a pie in this sentence? | The number about 3.14 used for circles | A small wooden box | *Food made of pastry filled with fruit, meat or vegetables | A round graph cut into slices
exercise: The class made a pie chart of their favourite fruits. What is a pie chart? | *A round graph cut into slices that show parts of a whole | A map of the school playground | A kind of pastry baked in an oven | A list of numbers in a row
exercise: Which word sounds the same as pie but means the number about 3.14 used for circles? | Pipe | Pier | Pine | *Pi
test: «Telling porkies» is British slang for what? | Running fast | Eating pies | Baking bread | *Telling lies
test: What do the slices of a pie chart show? | Words in a poem | The weather in May | Colours of the rainbow | *Parts of a whole, such as fractions or percentages
exam: In the phrase «as easy as pie», what does the phrase tell you? | It is a food from the oven | *It is very easy to do | It is a hard puzzle to solve | It is about baking bread
